Then define the operator precisely.

If “force alignment” means build the environment so strongly around the vision that people either opt in, adapt, or leave, that is ordinary leadership, institutional design, culture-building, incentives, standards, contracts, and selection. You can make the preferred path the easiest, most rewarded, most prestigious, or only compatible path without stripping people of basic agency.

If you mean coerce people through threats, intimidation, violence, blackmail, or deception, I won’t help with that. It is also strategically brittle: coerced agents defect, conceal, sabotage, and poison the feedback loop.

The durable version is:

Vision → rules → incentives → selection → reinforcement → culture → self-sustaining alignment.

You don’t need everyone to “believe.” You need the system to make aligned behavior locally rational, misaligned behavior costly or unrewarded, and exit possible. That is much stronger than trying to dominate each person directly.

Pantheon invariant: engineer the field, not the prisoner.